# Third-party licenses

The `eml` binary includes the third-party Rust crates listed below. eml itself is
distributed under the MIT License (`LICENSE.txt`); each crate here is distributed
under its own license, whose full text follows.

The list covers the crates `eml_cli` depends on with its default features and
the feature `gui-native`, on x86_64 and aarch64 Linux, aarch64 macOS and x86_64
Windows. Development and build dependencies are left out. Procedural macros and
their dependencies run only at compile time but remain in the list. Where a crate offers a choice of licenses,
the license text given is the one under which eml uses the crate. A crate that
ships no license file of its own is given the standard text of its license.

This file is generated by cargo-about from `about.toml` and `about.hbs`.
Do not edit it by hand; regenerate it with `nix develop -c nix/third-party-licenses.sh`.

## System libraries

With the feature `gui-native` (as `nix build` builds it), `eml` uses the
platform's GUI toolkit. The toolkit is not one of the crates below, and the
`eml` binary does not include it.

- Linux: Qt 6 (Qt Core, Qt Gui and Qt Widgets), by The Qt Company Ltd. and
  other contributors, used under the GNU Lesser General Public License
  version 3 (LGPL-3.0-only). `eml` links to the Qt libraries dynamically
  (`libQt6Core.so.6`, `libQt6Gui.so.6` and `libQt6Widgets.so.6`), so you may
  replace them with a modified version of Qt 6 and run `eml` with it. The
  source code of Qt is available from <https://download.qt.io/> and
  <https://code.qt.io/>. The LGPL version 3 adds permissions to the GNU
  General Public License version 3; the full texts of both licenses,
  `LGPL-3.0.txt` and `GPL-3.0.txt`, come with this file (in `share/doc/eml/`
  of the Nix package, and in `licenses/` of the source).
- Windows: the Windows App SDK runtime is Microsoft's. The user installs it
  separately from Microsoft, and `eml` includes none of its files.
- macOS: AppKit and Foundation are part of macOS.

